            <ArticleTitle>Occlusion of the left main coronary artery os by a tethered aortic valve cusp.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>e63-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.athoracsur.2013.10.108</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0003-4975(13)02488-0</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We report a 4-month-old girl who presented with poor ventricular function and was found to have occlusion of the left main coronary artery os by a tethered aortic cusp. The patient underwent surgical delamination of the aortic valve leaflet, revealing a normal left coronary os. After operative repair, her left ventricular function improved significantly. This diagnosis should be included in the differential in all children presenting with cardiac dysfunction.</AbstractText>
